---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import StatesSim from '../../simulators/states/StatesSim.astro';
import { statesQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/states';

const toc = [
  { id: 'model', label: 'The particle model' },
  { id: 'three', label: 'Solids, liquids and gases' },
  { id: 'temperature', label: 'Temperature and particle motion' },
  { id: 'changes', label: 'Changes of state' },
  { id: 'energy', label: 'Energy during a change of state' },
  { id: 'phase-diagram', label: 'Pressure and phase diagrams' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Chapter 10: Liquids and Solids (10.3 Phase Transitions; 10.4 Phase Diagrams). OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/10-3-phase-transitions' },
  { text: 'Flowers, P., et al. (2019). Chemistry 2e, Section 9.5: The Kinetic-Molecular Theory. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/9-5-the-kinetic-molecular-theory' },
  { text: 'Allen, M. P., & Tildesley, D. J. (2017). Computer Simulation of Liquids (2nd ed.). Oxford University Press. (Lennard-Jones molecular dynamics.)' },
  { text: 'NIST Chemistry WebBook: phase change data for water and carbon dioxide.', url: 'https://webbook.nist.gov/chemistry/' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="chemistry"
  topic="states-of-matter"
  description="Interactive states of matter simulator: heat and cool a simplified particle model and watch it melt, freeze, evaporate, boil and condense, with energy bars and a phase diagram."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={statesQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Ice, water and steam are the same substance. So what actually changes when ice melts or water boils? Heat and cool a
      container of particles and watch a regular solid loosen into a flowing liquid, then fly apart into a gas. Nothing
      in the model tells the particles which state to be in: it all comes from attraction competing with motion.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <StatesSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong>. The particles begin as a cold solid. Colours show their speed: blue is slow, red is fast.</li>
      <li>Drag the <strong>Temperature</strong> slider or press <strong>Solid</strong>, <strong>Liquid</strong> or <strong>Gas</strong>. Watch the arrangement, the spacing and the readouts change.</li>
      <li>Press a <strong>change of state</strong> button, such as <strong>Melting</strong> or <strong>Condensation</strong>, to watch the model heated or cooled slowly through that change. Read the note under the buttons.</li>
      <li>Switch <strong>Colour particles by</strong> to <strong>Arrangement</strong> to see which particles are solid-like, liquid-like or gas-like, and follow the graph below.</li>
      <li>Click a particle, the thermometer, the energy bars or a line on the phase diagram to learn more. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the cold solid.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="model">The particle model</h2>
  <p>
    All matter is made of tiny particles: atoms, molecules or ions. The <strong>particle model</strong> (also called the
    kinetic theory of matter) explains the states of matter with three ideas. The particles are always
    <strong>moving</strong>. They <strong>attract</strong> each other when close together, and repel strongly if pushed
    into each other. And the hotter the substance, the <strong>faster</strong> its particles move on average.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether a substance is a solid, liquid or gas depends on a contest between those attractions, which try to hold the
    particles together, and their motion, which tries to separate them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="three">Solids, liquids and gases</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Particles</th><th>Properties</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Solid</strong></td><td>Close together, usually in a regular pattern; vibrate about fixed places</td><td>Fixed shape and volume; hard to compress</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Liquid</strong></td><td>Close together, irregular; slide past each other</td><td>Flows and takes the shape of its container; fixed volume; hard to compress</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gas</strong></td><td>Far apart, random; move quickly in straight lines between collisions</td><td>Fills its container; easy to compress</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Notice in the simulator that the gap between neighbouring particles barely changes between solid and liquid, but jumps
    in the gas. That is why solids and liquids have similar densities, while a gas is far less dense.
  </p>

  <h2 id="temperature">Temperature and particle motion</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Temperature</strong> is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles. Not every particle moves at
    the same speed: at any temperature there is a range, from slow to fast, and collisions constantly swap energy between
    them. Click a few particles in the simulator to see their different speeds. Heating raises the average; cooling
    lowers it. At absolute zero (0 K, −273.15 °C) particle motion would be at its minimum.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes of state</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Melting</strong> (solid → liquid): particles vibrate so strongly that they break out of their fixed places.</li>
    <li><strong>Freezing</strong> (liquid → solid): particles slow down until attractions lock them into place.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaporation</strong> (liquid → gas at the surface): the fastest particles at the surface escape, at any temperature. Because the fastest particles leave, evaporation cools the liquid left behind.</li>
    <li><strong>Boiling</strong> (liquid → gas throughout): at the boiling point, particles throughout the liquid can escape, forming bubbles of gas inside it.</li>
    <li><strong>Condensation</strong> (gas → liquid): slow gas particles stick together when they meet, forming droplets.</li>
    <li><strong>Sublimation</strong> (solid → gas) and <strong>deposition</strong> (gas → solid) skip the liquid state entirely.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These are <strong>physical changes</strong>: the particles themselves are not changed, and no new substance is made.
    Melting ice and freezing water are the same water molecules, just arranged differently.
  </p>

  <h2 id="energy">Energy during a change of state</h2>
  <p>
    Heat a pure solid and its temperature rises until it starts to melt. Then something surprising happens: the
    temperature stays almost constant until all of it has melted. The energy supplied is not making the particles faster;
    it is being used to <strong>overcome the attractions</strong> between them. This energy is called
    <strong>latent heat</strong>. The same happens during boiling, which needs much more energy because the particles must
    be separated completely. Water needs about 334 joules to melt each gram of ice, but about 2,260 joules to boil each
    gram of water. When a gas condenses or a liquid freezes, the same energy is released again.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two energy bars in the simulator show this: the red bar is the motion energy (temperature), and the green bar is
    the energy stored by pulling particles apart. Watch the green bar jump during melting and especially during boiling.
  </p>

  <h2 id="phase-diagram">Pressure and phase diagrams</h2>
  <p>
    State depends on <strong>pressure</strong> as well as temperature. Higher pressure pushes particles together, so it
    favours the denser states. A <strong>phase diagram</strong> maps which state a substance is in at every temperature
    and pressure. The lines mark the conditions where two states can exist together. At the <strong>triple point</strong>
    all three coexist; below that pressure, a solid cannot melt, and it sublimes instead. For water the triple point is
    0.01 °C at about 0.6% of normal air pressure, so ice normally melts. For carbon dioxide it is −56.6 °C at about 5
    times normal air pressure, so dry ice sublimes on a kitchen table. This is also why water boils at only about 70 °C
    on top of Mount Everest, where the air pressure is about a third of that at sea level.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Kinetic energy</dt>
    <dd>Energy of motion. The average kinetic energy of the particles sets the temperature.</dd>
    <dt>Melting point</dt>
    <dd>The temperature at which a solid turns into a liquid (and a liquid freezes) at a given pressure.</dd>
    <dt>Boiling point</dt>
    <dd>The temperature at which a liquid boils throughout at a given pressure.</dd>
    <dt>Latent heat</dt>
    <dd>Energy taken in or given out during a change of state without a change in temperature.</dd>
    <dt>Triple point</dt>
    <dd>The single temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid and gas can coexist.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sweating:</strong> evaporation of sweat removes the fastest water molecules and cools your skin.</li>
    <li><strong>Refrigerators</strong> pump a fluid that evaporates inside (absorbing heat) and condenses outside (releasing it).</li>
    <li><strong>Pressure cookers</strong> raise the boiling point of water above 100 °C so food cooks faster.</li>
    <li><strong>Freeze-drying</strong> foods and medicines removes ice by sublimation at low pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Metal casting:</strong> metals are melted, poured into moulds and frozen into shape.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Particles in a solid don’t move.”</strong> They vibrate constantly; they just stay in place.</li>
    <li><strong>“Particles expand when heated.”</strong> The particles stay the same size; the spaces between them change.</li>
    <li><strong>“Evaporation only happens at the boiling point.”</strong> It happens at any temperature, more slowly when cold.</li>
    <li><strong>“The bubbles in boiling water are air.”</strong> They are mostly water vapour.</li>
    <li><strong>“Changing state is a chemical reaction.”</strong> It is a physical change; no new substance forms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    The simulator is a small <strong>molecular dynamics</strong> model: 100 identical round particles in two dimensions,
    moving by Newton’s laws with a standard Lennard-Jones attraction and repulsion. A thermostat plays the role of the
    heater, and weak gravity keeps the liquid at the bottom. Temperatures are in model units, chosen so that this model
    melts at about 35 and boils at about 70; they are not degrees Celsius and do not describe a particular substance.
    With so few particles, changes of state are spread over a range of temperatures rather than happening at one sharp
    temperature, boiling does not form proper bubbles, and the temperature does not stay perfectly constant during a
    change. Sublimation is real but far too slow to see in a model this small, so it is shown on the schematic phase
    diagram instead. Real molecules have shapes, and some (like water) form hydrogen bonds, which is why ice is less
    dense than liquid water: something this model cannot show.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>States of matter differ in particle arrangement and motion, not in the particles themselves.</li>
    <li>Temperature measures the average kinetic energy of the particles, and speeds vary around that average.</li>
    <li>A change of state happens when particle motion overcomes, or gives in to, the attractions between particles.</li>
    <li>During melting and boiling, energy goes into separating particles, so the temperature stays nearly constant.</li>
    <li>Pressure also controls state; below the triple point a solid sublimes instead of melting.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
